-- title:
--   q-expansion pin of the specialisation on the Gauss component
-- statement:
--   Setting. Fix a prime $p$, a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, an integer $M$ with $M \ge 5$ and $p \nmid M$, a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a $\{p\}$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$, and an intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}(L)$ with $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Fix a discrete valuation domain $A$ with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$, with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly, and an element $j \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the coefficient embedding of the $j$-series [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), assumed non-zero. The ambient model is the two-chart model [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229), the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the integral closures of $A[j]$ and $A[j^{-1}]$ in $K$, together with its structure morphism [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, assumed proper. The residue field $\kappa =$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField ↥Pl` is assumed algebraically closed of characteristic $p$ and an $A$-algebra.
--
--   Groups of hypotheses. The statement carries a long list of data and assumptions, which are grouped as follows; each group is summarised, and none is passed over.
--
--   (1) Special fibre by two components: schemes $C_1, C_2$ proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral over $\kappa$, closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ of them into the base change of the two-chart model to $\kappa$, the hypothesis `hcover` that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$, reducedness `hred` of the scheme-theoretic intersection $C_1 \times_{X_\kappa} C_2$, and a positive integer $n$ with `hn` equating $n$ to the number of points of that intersection.
--
--   (2) Sections: a section $\varepsilon$ of the two-chart model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, sections $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ of $c_1, c_2$ over $\kappa$, and `hε₁` saying that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $\kappa$.
--
--   (3) Relative Picard data: a designation $D$ (a scheme $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ with a zero section) together with `hrep`, the non-empty assertion that $D$ represents the subfunctor of rigidified line bundles on the two-chart model which are fibrewise algebraically equivalent to zero, smoothness `hsm` and separatedness `hsep` of $D.toBase$; the analogous representability `hreps` over $\kappa$ for the base-changed designation, the comparison `hPk` between the Poincaré bundle over $\kappa$ and the base change of the Poincaré bundle of $D$; designations $D_1, D_2$ over $\kappa$ for $c_1, c_2$ with representability `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`; a morphism $\nu_2$ from the base change of $D$ to $D_2$ over $\kappa$ and the compatibility `hν₂` identifying, for every $\kappa$-scheme $T$ and every $T$-point $a$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of $D_2$ along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ with the rigidification along $\varepsilon_2$ of the pullback of the corresponding bundle to $C_2$.
--
--   (4) Geometric fibres over $L$ and $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$: $A$-algebra structures on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ compatible with $L$; smoothness `hsmL` and geometric integrality `hgiL` of the base change of the model to $L$; properness `hprL` and geometric connectedness `hgcL` of $D.P \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec} L \to \operatorname{Spec} L$; a curve model $M_\eta$ of the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, an isomorphism $e_\eta$ of $M_\eta.C$ with the base change of the two-chart model to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over the base (`heη`), non-emptiness of the preimage of the finite chart, and the pinning `hMηpin` which reads the function-field element attached to each element $a$ of the finite chart algebra as the coefficientwise image of the Laurent expansion of $a$.
--
--   (5) Galois and Hecke structure: `hgal`, which for every $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ fixing $L$ pointwise expresses the place attached to a twisted $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $M_\eta.C$ as the image of the place of the original point under the arithmetic Galois action [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54); the Hecke input hypotheses `hin` (`HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`) and the commutation hypothesis `hcomm` (`HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`); a semiring action of $\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ on $A$ with compatibility `hΓA` with the structure map $A \to L$.
--
--   (6) Special fibre group datum: a `NeronSpecialFibreGeom p` datum $G$, consisting of abelian groups $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$, a subgroup $G.torus$ and a surjection $G.proj : G.J0s \to G.JI \times G.JE$ with kernel $G.torus$; bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` of $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$ with the $\kappa$-points of the base-changed $D$, of $D_1$, of $D_2$; additivity of the three bijections in the Poincaré-bundle sense (`hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE`); the compatibility `hproj` of $G.proj$ with the morphisms induced by $i_1$ and by $\nu_2$; and `hPTS`, which states that `pts` is additive and unital for the relative group law of `hreps`.
--
--   (7) Generic points, Hecke correspondences and Galois twists on $D$: a bijection `gpts` of $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}) =$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) with the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D.toBase$, a family $\varphi$ of endomorphisms of $D.toBase$ indexed by the Hecke algebra [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16), a family $\tau$ of semilinear automorphisms indexed by $\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$, and the hypotheses `hφmul` (each $\varphi(t)$ is additive for the relative group law), `hφpts` (compatibility of $\varphi$ with the Hecke module structure via `gpts`), `hτ1`, `hτmul` (the $\tau$ form an action), `hτφ` (the $\tau$ commute with the $\varphi$), `hgadd` (`gpts` is additive), `hτpts` (Galois equivariance of `gpts`).
--
--   (8) The Abel–Jacobi description over $L$ and over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$: the representability `hDL` over $L$, an Abel–Jacobi morphism `ajL` from the base-changed curve to the base-changed designation, a comparison morphism `kL` of base changes with `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`, a morphism `ajbar` from $M_\eta.C$ to $D.P$ with `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point `εbar` with `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, the bundle comparison `hPL`, the normalisation `hajLε` ($\varepsilon$ goes to the zero section), the divisorial characterisation `hajL` of `ajL` (the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along a point is the line bundle of the point's graph divisor tensored with the ideal module of the section), and `hpts_aj`, which says that for $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$ and $s$ with $s$ lying over $\varepsilon$ there is a degree-zero divisor with divisor class $\delta_{[x]} - \delta_{[s]}$ whose image under `gpts` is $x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   (9) The special fibre operators: a `NeronSpecialFibreOpsV3 G` datum $O$, and the hypothesis `hO` (four clauses) that the Hecke operators, the two kinds of diamond operators and the inertia operators of $O$ are computed, under `pts`, by the corresponding $\varphi(\cdot)$ respectively by $\tau(s)$ for $s$ acting on $\zeta$ by $\zeta \mapsto \zeta^{b}$.
--
--   (10) Integral models of points over inertia-fixed subrings: $Pl$ lies over $p$ (`hPl`), a ring homomorphism $\rho : A \to Pl$ with `hρ` and `hAlgκ` compatibilities, and the hypothesis `hF3`, which for each subgroup $I$ of $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ contained in the inertia subgroup at $Pl$, acting trivially on $p$-th roots of unity and of finite index in that inertia subgroup, produces a lift of $A$ into $\mathcal{O}_I = Pl \cap \overline{\mathbb{Q}}^{I}$ and asserts five clauses about the set $\mathrm{dom}$ of classes whose $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point factors through $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}_I$: $I$-invariance, injectivity of restriction of $\mathcal{O}_I$-points, that $\mathrm{dom}$ contains $0$ and is closed under differences, injectivity and surjectivity of reduction on $n$-torsion for $n$ prime to $p$, and stability of $\mathrm{dom}$ under Frobenius elements normalising $I$.
--
--   (11) The Néron-type subscheme: a scheme $\mathcal{A}$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ with a morphism $\iota$ to $D.toBase$ and the hypothesis `h𝒜` (six clauses) that $\iota$ is a closed immersion, $\mathcal{A}$ is proper and smooth over $A$ with connected geometric fibres, that $\mathcal{A}$ is closed under the unit, multiplication and inversion of the relative group law, that the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $\mathcal{A}$ are exactly the classes in the norm-free part [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29), and that $\mathcal{A}$ is stable under all $\varphi(t)$; together with `hF10`, stating that on points of the base change of $D$ to a $\kappa$-algebra which factor through $\mathcal{A}$, the operator $\varphi$ of the sum of diamond generators over $(\mathbb{Z}/p)^\times$ (indexed through the Chinese remainder isomorphism) acts as the identity element of the relative group law.
--
--   (12) The Igusa picture of the special fibre: an integral weight-one form $w$ over $\kappa$ of level $M$, curve models $\mathrm{Mdl}_1, \mathrm{Mdl}_2$ over $\kappa$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35) (the subfield of $\kappa((q))$ generated by the mod-$p$ $q$-expansion field of $X_1(M)$ and the inverse of the reduced $q$-expansion of $w$), isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$, $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ over $\kappa$ (`he₁`, `he₂`), the Gauss reading `hgauss₁` of the function field of $C_1$: for every $a$ in the finite chart algebra and every pair of power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $\bar y \neq 0$ and $a \cdot y = x$ in $L((q))$, the element of the Igusa field attached to $a$ on $C_1$ has Laurent expansion $\bar x / \bar y$; the hypothesis `hεC₂` that the reduced section avoids $C_2$; the hypothesis `hεgal`, that $\varepsilon$ is equivariant for any automorphism of the two-chart model inducing a given coefficientwise Galois action on the chart algebra; a finite set `nodesIg` of pairs of places of the Igusa field; additive isomorphisms $\Psi : G.J0s \cong$ `GluedPic0 κ (Igusa field) nodesIg`, $\theta_1 : G.JI \cong \mathrm{Pic}^0$, $\theta_2 : G.JE \cong \mathrm{Pic}^0$; the hypothesis `hF4c` (seven clauses) that `nodesIg` is exactly the set of pairs of places cut out by the intersection points of $C_1$ and $C_2$, has cardinality $n$ and injective projections, that $\Psi$ followed by `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair` is $(\theta_1, \theta_2) \circ G.proj$, that $\Psi$ carries $G.torus$ onto the range of `GluedPic0.nodeUnit`, and that $\theta_1$, $\theta_2$ are computed by the divisor $\delta_x - \delta_{\varepsilon_i}$ on the appropriate component; and a semilinear automorphism `frobT` of the Igusa field raising all Laurent coefficients to the $p$-th power (`hfrobT`).
--
--   (13) The abstract specialisation family: for each admissible $I$ as in (10), a subgroup $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}(I) \le J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ and an additive map $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}(I)$ from it to `GluedPic0 κ (Igusa field) nodesIg`, subject to `hIFm` (two clauses: membership in $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}(I)$ is equivalent to the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $D$ factoring through $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}_I$, and $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}(I)$ of such a class is $\Psi$ applied to the $\kappa$-point obtained by reduction of that $\mathcal{O}_I$-point) and to `hlev` (two clauses: a push-out along $J \le I$ of integral points, and independence of $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}$ under shrinking $I$ to $J$).
--
--   Conclusion. For every subgroup $I$ of $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ contained in the inertia subgroup at $Pl$, acting trivially on the $p$-th roots of unity and of finite index in that inertia subgroup, the following holds. Let
--   $$r_1 : \mathrm{Place}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}, \mathrm{laurentBaseChange}\text{ of } X_1(Mp/p)) \to \mathrm{Place}(\kappa, \mathrm{x1FunctionFieldC}\text{ of } X_1(Mp/p))$$
--   be a map of places which is a Laurent place reduction for $Pl$ and the residue map $Pl \to \kappa$ (degree-preserving, and compatible with the order functions of integral Laurent elements and their reductions), and assume that the principal divisors of the level-$Mp/p$ base-changed field are generated by the divisors of integral Laurent elements. Let $\iota$ be an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra map from the level $Mp/p$ base-changed function field into the level $Mp$ one which is integral and a $q$-expansion inclusion (it does not change Laurent expansions), and let $\bar\iota$ be an integral $\kappa$-algebra map from the level $Mp/p$ mod-$p$ $q$-expansion field into the Igusa field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35) which is likewise a $q$-expansion inclusion. Let $D_1$ and $D$ be degree-zero divisors on the level $Mp/p$ and level $Mp$ base-changed fields respectively, with $D$ the conorm of $D_1$ along $\iota$, that is $D(w) = e_w \cdot D_1(w|_{F})$ for every place $w$, and assume that the class $\mathrm{Pic}^0.\mathrm{mk}(D)$ lies in $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}(I)$. Finally let $\bar D$ be a degree-zero divisor on the Igusa field which is the conorm along $\bar\iota$ of the pushforward $\mathrm{mapDomain}(r_1)(D_1)$ of $D_1$ under $r_1$.
--
--   Then the first component of the image of $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}(I)\big(\mathrm{Pic}^0.\mathrm{mk}(D)\big)$ under `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair nodesIg` equals $\mathrm{Pic}^0.\mathrm{mk}(\bar D)$.
--
--   This is the $q$-expansion pinning clause for the specialisation family attached to the norm-free part of $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$: on the component of the special fibre read by $q$-expansions (the Gauss component, identified with an Igusa curve), the specialisation map is computed divisorwise by reduction of places and conorm along the degeneracy map from level $Mp/p$. It feeds the assembly of a pinned semistable specialisation datum with diamond operators, which is the input to the level-lowering step.
